// Formative practice quiz for accessible-forms. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design. First draft, not yet reviewed.
$CATEGORY: react/forms-context-and-crud/accessible-forms

::accessible-forms o1 - htmlFor::In JSX, how do you associate a label with an input whose id is email?{
~Give the label for set to email#Not quite. for is the HTML name. JSX uses htmlFor.
~Give the label id set to email#Not quite. Two elements cannot share an id, and this creates no association.
=Give the label htmlFor set to email#Correct. htmlFor is the JSX name for the for attribute.
~Put the label text in the placeholder#Not quite. A placeholder is not a label.
}

::accessible-forms o1 - label benefit::Besides naming the field for screen readers, what does an associated label do?{
=Clicking the label focuses the field#Correct. This gives everyone a larger target.
~It validates the input#Not quite. A label does not validate anything.
~It submits the form#Not quite. Clicking a label for a text field does not submit.
~It stores the value#Not quite. State or the input stores the value.
}

::accessible-forms o2 - describedby::What does aria-describedby on an input refer to?{
~The text of the error itself#Not quite. It holds an id reference, not the text.
=The id of the element holding its description, such as an error message#Correct. The referenced text is announced along with the field.
~The name of the form#Not quite. It refers to an element by id.
~A CSS class#Not quite. It has nothing to do with styling.
}

::accessible-forms o2 - aria-invalid timing::When should an input have aria-invalid set to true?{
~Always, on required fields#Not quite. An empty required field that has not been checked is not yet in error.
=Only while it has a validation error#Correct. It should reflect the current state of the field.
~Never, since required already covers it#Not quite. required states a rule, and aria-invalid reports the current problem.
~Only on the submit button#Not quite. It belongs on the field in error.
}

::accessible-forms o3 - form-level result::A form fails on submission and an error appears at the top of the page. How do screen reader users learn about it?{
~Make the text red#Not quite. Color does not get announced.
~Nothing is needed, because they will read the page again#Not quite. They have no way of knowing that something changed.
~Add a title attribute to the page#Not quite. That does not announce anything.
=Put it in a live region or move focus to it#Correct. Either one gets the message announced.
}
